::Q1::What is the main reason a local cloud emulator is used instead of a real cloud account for practice and automated testing? 
{
=It responds to the same commands without creating billable cloud resources
~It runs the same code significantly faster than real AWS
~It provides a graphical console that is easier to learn than the AWS one
~It automatically corrects mistakes in AWS commands
}

::Q2::Why do the AWS CLI commands written in the earlier modules run against Floci without being rewritten? 
{
=Floci speaks the same protocol as AWS, so only the endpoint the commands are sent to changes
~The AWS CLI detects that Floci is running and converts commands automatically
~Floci ships a modified version of the AWS CLI that understands both
~The commands are rewritten by Docker before reaching Floci
}

::Q3::When configuring the AWS CLI to talk to Floci, why can the access key and secret key be any placeholder text such as "test"? 
{
=Nothing is authenticating against real AWS, so the credential values are not checked against a real account
~The AWS CLI ignores credentials whenever an endpoint URL is set
~Floci generates real temporary AWS credentials in the background
~Placeholder credentials grant read only access as a safety measure
}

::Q4::A learner sets the four environment variables, runs some commands successfully, then opens a new terminal window where the same commands begin failing. What is the most likely cause? 
{
=The variables apply only to the terminal session where they were set, and must be set again in the new one
~Floci stopped running when the first terminal lost focus
~The emulator only permits one terminal to connect at a time
~The bucket created earlier expired automatically
}

::Q5::A learner creates several buckets, stops the emulator with docker compose down, then starts it again and finds the buckets are gone. How should this be interpreted? 
{
=Expected behavior, since everything created inside Floci is discarded by default when the container stops
~A defect that should be reported, since resources should always persist
~Evidence that the buckets were created in real AWS instead
~A sign that Docker is misconfigured
}

::Q6::Because a local emulator supports the core operations of each service, experience with it fully replaces the need for any exposure to real AWS. 
{FALSE}